course: dictionary
lesson: 4368
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Gutter names a pipe on a roof, a channel at the edge of a street, and a channel beside a bowling lane. It can also be a verb about a candle flame. The sentence around the word tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «She climbed onto the roof to look around, then slipped on the gutter and broke her arm.» The roof is the clue. Gutter here means the pipe along the edge of the roof, not the street. Ask yourself where the action happens, and the place will point you to the right meaning.
explain: «A fast-flowing gutter or ditch is much stronger than it looks.» Here the clues are fast water and a ditch, so gutter means the low channel at the edge of a street that carries rainwater to drains. Try each meaning in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and this is called multiple meaning.
fact: A gutter is a long open pipe along the edge of a roof that carries rainwater away.
fact: A gutter is also the low channel at the edge of a street that takes rainwater to drains.
fact: In bowling, a gutter is the channel along the side of the lane, and a gutter ball misses all the pins.
fact: As a verb, a candle guttered when its flame flickered and almost went out. Rainwater runs from roofs through gutters and drains to rivers, and blocked gutters can overflow.
exercise: «She slipped on the gutter and broke her arm.» Where was the gutter in this sentence? | Inside a candle | Under the sea | In the bowling alley | *On the roof, where she had climbed
exercise: Sam says, «The candle guttered and went out.» What does guttered mean? | To drip wax onto the table | *To flicker and almost go out | To fall over with a crash | To burn very brightly
exercise: Where would you find the low channel that takes rainwater to drains? | In a dry cupboard | Inside a bowling ball | At the top of a candle | *At the edge of a street
test: In bowling, what is a gutter ball? | A ball made of glass | A ball that knocks down every pin | A ball that rolls off the table | *A ball that misses all the pins
test: Why should you never play near street gutters and drains in heavy rain? | They are full of sweets | Rain makes them grow bigger | They are too warm to touch | *Fast water in them is much stronger than it looks
exam: Which meaning of gutter is used in «clean the gutters» after a storm? | A channel along a bowling lane | A very poor way of living | *A pipe along the edge of a roof that carries rainwater away | To flicker and almost go out
